 Member 3 (**Pediatric Cardiology Specialist**): Evaluates the patient’s holosystolic murmur and overall cardiac health. This member will assess possible cardiac involvement associated with syndromic conditions, particularly given the physical examination findings suggestive of potential underlying pathology.

Group 3 -  Diagnostic Evidence Team (DET)
 Member 1 (**Ophthalmologist (Lead)**): Performs detailed ocular examinations and aims to correlate the findings of multinodular, calcified lesions in the retina with potential associated syndromes like Tuberous Sclerosis. The ophthalmologist's expertise is crucial for interpreting fundoscopic findings and determining their implications for the diagnosis.
 Member 2 (**Radiologist**): Specializes in imaging analysis and will review any necessary imaging studies (e.g., CT, MRI) to look for signs of any neurological tumors or calcifications that correspond with possible genetic syndromes. The radiologist aids in examining anatomical structures and assessing any further complications.
 Member 3 (**Clinical Geneticist/Counselor**): Provides essential genetic testing for specific mutations associated with the syndromic features observed in the patient (e.g., mutations in TSC1 or TSC2 for Tuberous Sclerosis). They will discuss genetic testing options and implications with the family.

Group 4 -  Final Review and Decision Team (FRDT)
 Member 1 (**Senior Neurologist (Lead)**): Provides overarching neurological expertise, guiding the final interpretation of the assessment data and findings. The senior neurologist will synthesize information from the Initial Assessment and Diagnostic Evidence teams to finalize the diagnosis and management plan.
 Member 2 (**Pediatrician**): Offers insight into the overall care and management of the patient within the pediatric context. The pediatrician will coordinate care following diagnosis, including developmental support and monitoring for the associated issues related to the suspected genetic disorder.
